  "problem": "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)=2x+1$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x-1.$",
  "worked_solution": "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\u2032} & {y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{\u2032}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{\u2032}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=2x+1$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x-1.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(2x+1){(x-1)}^{\u2032}-(x-1){(2x+1)}^{\u2032} \\\\  & =(2x+1)(1)-(x-1)(2) \\\\  & =2x+1-2x+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
